    "title": "UNMH critical care tower officials outline departments on each floor - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Nicole Sanders No media credit provided. ,\n1 hours ago\nALBUQUERQUE, N.M. (KRQE) — UNMH is set to open its new critical care tower next month and developers say the six-story facility will transform patient care in New Mexico. Here is a look at how the departments will be distributed.\nStory continues below\nNews: McKinley County DA has 30 days to respond to removal petition Trending: VIDEO: Woman, child, dog saved in swift water rescue in Grant County News: Conversion of Albuquerque intersections to four-way-stops is working city says New Mexico: Governor signs off on $1.5 million for flood assistance in Mora County On the ground floor, a new emergency department will have 68 beds and will serve only adult patients. The current pediatric emergency center will stay where it is and expand to have 29 more beds. The first floor will house admitting, imaging, and Native American health services.\nThe second floor covers pharmacy, lab work, a cafe, and a spiritual health room. The third floor focuses on surgery, interventional radiology and pre-surgery prep. The fourth is a sky campus that supports staff training and education. The fifth floor houses the trauma and neuroscience ICU while the sixth floor is intensive care for patients with critical heart, chest, and blood vessel conditions.\nThe critical care tower will open to patients Oct. 5.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KRQE NEWS 13 - Breaking News, Albuquerque News, New Mexico News, Weather, and Videos.\n1",
